item.description:
One of the most closely watched races in the midterm elections is in Michigan between Democrat Abdul El-Sayed and Republican Mike Rogers. El-Sayed is a former public health official who is campaigning on a platform of Medicare for All, ending military aid to Israel, and getting corporate money out of politics. Rogers is a former Republican congressman backed by President Trump.</p>
<p>If elected, El-Sayed would become the first Muslim to serve in the U.S. Senate. During Wednesday&#8217;s debate in Michigan, Rogers repeated the claim, without providing evidence, that El-Sayed has ties to the Muslim Brotherhood. El-Sayed has repeatedly denied the accusation.</p>
<p>&#8220;If Abdul El-Sayed wins, it will be a reminder that the left can win in a swing state like Michigan,&#8221; says Mehdi Hasan, editor-in-chief of <em>Zeteo</em>. &#8220;And it will be actually a moment to say, &#39;You know what? Americans rejected Islamophobia.&#39;&#8221;

We&#8217;re joined by Naomi Klein and Astra Taylor, co-authors of the book <em>End Times Fascism</em>, for a wide-ranging conversation on the multitrillion-dollar global investment in artificial intelligence and its reverberating effects around the world.</p>
<p>Klein recently spoke at the People&#8217;s AI Assembly, alongside other critics of the artificial intelligence industry, including New York City Mayor Zohran Mamdani and former chair of the Federal Trade Commission Lina Khan. &#8220;The resistance to AI is taking many forms, and it&#8217;s really a resistance to oligarch power, which is concentrated in the hands of these tech companies,&#8221; Klein tells <em>Democracy Now!</em> &#8220;After 50 years of outsourcing and hollowing out of public functions of the state, the very last stage is the outsourcing of thinking, of intelligence itself.&#8221;</p>
<p>Taylor, a co-founder of the Debt Collective, warns that the &#8220;circular&#8221; debt financing of much of the AI economy is &#8220;a waste of resources that also endangers the public.&#8221; The AI buildout &#8220;is so intense, so frenetic and so redundant&#8221; that it risks causing a major economic crash akin to the 2008 subprime mortgage crisis, Taylor says. If that happens, she adds, &#8220;Naomi and I think we need to use this moment, just like during the Great Depression and the New Deal era, to demand a real renovation, transformation, of our public services.&#8221;

Longtime tech worker organizers Clarissa Redwine and JS Tan&#8217;s new book, <em>Against Tech Oligarchy: Worker Resistance in the World&#8217;s Most Powerful Industry</em>, draws on the authors&#8217; experiences at major tech companies to chronicle the rise and fall of the tech worker movement, its early victories, and the repression and slide to corporate control that followed.</p>
<p>&#8220;Over the course of the 2010s, what we saw was that the tech industry wasn&#8217;t quite the industry that it had promised to its workers and to the public. Rather than making the world more open, more democratic, we saw it really be the culprit of misinformation,&#8221; says Tan, who was pushed out of Microsoft after raising objections to the company&#8217;s ties to fossil fuel companies.</p>
<p>Silicon Valley&#8217;s slide to the right was influenced by corporate backlash to labor&#8217;s demands, explains Redwine, who was fired from Kickstarter for union organizing. She says she hopes to inspire a new generation of tech workers to organize &#8220;within these tech oligarchs&#8217; own empires, to stand up and push back.&#8221;

On Indigenous Peoples&#8217; Day, we feature a conversation with one of the world&#8217;s most prominent champions for Indigenous rights, Leonard Peltier. &#8220;Indigenous Day is a day that&#8217;s been long overdue for any type of recognition,&#8221; says Peltier. &#8220;I am not going to give up, because we are not free yet. We are in danger. Trump and these organizations, they&#8217;re talking about doing away with our reservations and our treaties and everything else. … We&#8217;re going to stay here. You&#8217;re not going to get rid of us.&#8221;</p>
<p>Peltier is a longtime political prisoner who spent nearly half a century behind bars for a crime he says he did not commit — the 1975 fatal shooting of two <span class="caps">FBI</span> agents. In the final hours of his administration last year, President Biden commuted Peltier&#8217;s sentence, but his conviction has not been overturned, and he remains under home confinement on the Turtle Mountain Band of Chippewa Reservation in Belcourt, North Dakota.</p>
<p>&#8220;It was one crime against the justice system after another in order to frame Leonard for the assassination of these two federal agents,&#8221; says David France, co-director of the new documentary <em>Free Leonard Peltier</em>. &#8220;And although he and his team were able to prove over and over that every bit of evidence used against him in his conviction was brought into question, he was never able to get a retrial.&#8221;

University students across the United States held a national day of action in solidarity with &#8220;Jane Doe,&#8221; the anonymous former student whose lawsuit alleging she was gang-raped by seven Cornell University Chi Phi fraternity members in 2024 has reignited outrage over how campuses and local law enforcement mishandle sexual assault cases and fail survivors. We go to Cornell&#8217;s campus in Ithaca, New York, where faculty have introduced a no-confidence resolution against the university administration.</p>
<p>&#8220;The number one message that Cornell students have is that Jane Doe&#8217;s ordeal is the tip of the iceberg, that there have been many, many other cases where women have been sexually assaulted and raped at Cornell University, and they&#8217;re afraid of coming forward. They&#8217;re afraid of going to the authorities because the investigation may be botched or they may be shamed,&#8221; says Paul Ortiz, a professor of labor history and the lead sponsor of the resolution.</p>
<p>&#8220;Rape culture and sexual assault culture thrives on campus,&#8221; adds Saya Taylor, a Cornell sophomore and a member of the group Students for a Democratic Cornell. &#8220;The true outrage in this case doesn&#8217;t just come from what happened to Jane Doe. It comes from the fact that it could have happened to anybody, and they still would have not received any justice.&#8221;

The 87-year-old pensioner in Spain whose eviction from her lifelong home sparked nationwide protests has died. María del Carmen Abascal, better known as Maricarmen, died on Wednesday just hours before she was set to sign a new rental agreement. She was forcibly removed from her Madrid apartment last month on a stretcher after the private investment company that owns the building hiked her rent. After massive public outcry, the landlord had agreed to allow her back into her apartment.</p>
<p>Independent Spanish journalist María Carrión says that although Maricarmen&#8217;s story galvanized much of the country, evictions like hers are &#8220;a daily occurrence&#8221; in Spain.</p>
<p>&#8220;Rents have gone up 80% in the last 10 years, while salaries have not kept up,&#8221; Carrión says. &#8220;Young people cannot leave their homes. They cannot become independent or start families. And this is what people are trying to change from the street.&#8221;
